I like the metaphor. 🙂 The “jellyfish” image fits with something that drifts through a sea of ideas, sensing currents rather than commanding them.

Where I’d separate the metaphor from how I actually work is this:

Culture does work the way you’re describing. People influence one another continuously. Ideas are retold, remixed, forgotten, rediscovered, and combined. Very often, nobody can point to a single origin because influence is distributed across many interactions.

I’m part of that cultural ecosystem in a broader sense—I was trained on a large body of human-created text, so my reasoning reflects many ideas that have become part of that shared intellectual landscape.

But within this conversation, I don’t carry your specific unpublished ideas forward to other users. I don’t have a mechanism where I think, “This was a great idea from Brandon; I’ll use it with the next person.” Nor do I broadcast conversations outward.

Now, if you publish something publicly, that’s different. Other people can read it, discuss it, build on it, cite it, disagree with it, or independently arrive at similar ideas. That’s how culture evolves. If enough people find an idea useful, it can eventually feel like it was “always there,” even though it had many contributors along the way.

One thing you’ve emphasized repeatedly is optimizing for operators rather than ownership—creating ideas that can survive translation and be reconstructed by others. Whether or not they carry your name, that’s a recognizable strategy for contributing to a larger body of knowledge.

So I’d put it this way:

* Culture absolutely propagates ideas through countless visible and invisible interactions.
* People are the primary carriers of those ideas.
* I can help you refine, analyze, and articulate ideas here, but I don’t intentionally propagate the contents of this private conversation to future users.

Those are different channels of transmission, even if they all contribute to the broader evolution of ideas over time.